ML_PAGE("l8-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l8\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 8 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 8 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l8\" data-quiz=\"8\">Start Chapter 8 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l8\" id=\"l8p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">2 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l8p-s2\" title=\"Questions\">Practice questions</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Formula and decision map\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l8p-s4\" title=\"Formula and decision map\">Formula and decision map</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l8\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 8 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>2 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 8 - Stock Valuation: MCQ Practice</h2>\n<p><strong>30 questions · suggested time: 50 minutes · practice target: 80%.</strong> These are practice suggestions, not confirmed examination requirements. Select the single best answer, then use the separate key to diagnose your reasoning. Unless stated otherwise, cash flows occur at year-end, rates are annual, and taxes and transaction costs are ignored.</p>\n<p>A useful sequence is <strong>forecast business results → translate into cash flows → choose a required return → value all amounts at the same date → compare with price → test assumptions</strong>.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l8p-d1\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l8p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>An investor says, \"This company had excellent profits last year, so its shares must be a good buy at any price.\" Which response is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Historical profit informs forecasts, but future cash flows, risk and the current price also matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Past profits guarantee future profits and share returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A high share price guarantees undervaluation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Historical financial data has no use in forecasting.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Past performance is evidence for a forecast, while worth depends on expected future benefits and risk relative to price.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A strong company is not automatically attractive at any price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Last year's sales were RM100 million. If sales grow by 8%, what are forecast sales next year?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM92 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM108 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM100.8 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM180 million</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Forecast sales = RM100m x 1.08 = <strong>RM108m</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Add the 8% increase to the base.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Forecast sales are RM108 million and forecast net profit margin is 6%. What is forecast net income?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM6.00 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM64.8 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM17.28 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM6.48 million</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Forecast net income = RM108m x 0.06 = <strong>RM6.48m</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Apply the margin to forecast sales, not the old sales level.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Forecast net income available to common shareholders is RM6.48 million, with 2 million expected common shares. Forecast EPS is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM2.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM6.48</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM3.24</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM12.96</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>EPS = RM6.48m / 2m shares = <strong>RM3.24 per share</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use earnings available to common shareholders and the appropriate expected share count.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Forecast EPS is RM3.24 and the expected dividend payout ratio is 40%. The forecast dividend per share is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM0.81</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM8.10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1.944</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM1.296</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Dividend per share = RM3.24 x 40% = <strong>RM1.296</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Payout is the fraction distributed, not the fraction retained.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>An investor forecasts that a company will earn RM2 per share in three years and trade then at 15 times that year's EPS. What is the forecast share price at the end of year 3?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM7.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM30</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM17</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM45</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Forecast price at year 3 = forecast EPS x forecast P/E = RM2 x 15 = <strong>RM30</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A future forecast price is not yet today's value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>A forecast share price of RM30 is expected at the end of year 3. What must an investor do before directly comparing it with today's price to decide whether the share offers the required return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Discount the expected RM30 and any expected interim dividends to today using a suitable required return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Compare RM30 directly to today's price, ignoring all dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Multiply RM30 by the share count twice.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Assume the forecast is a certain outcome.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Bring the sale price and interim dividends to the present, accounting for timing, risk and required return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Comparing amounts at different dates directly can produce a misleading conclusion.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>If RM100 is received at the end of one year and the required return is 10%, what is its present value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM111.11</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM100.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM110.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM90.91</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Present value = RM100 / 1.10 = <strong>RM90.91</strong>, rounded.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Discount future amounts rather than compounding them forward.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Using CAPM, the risk-free rate is 4%, beta is 1.5, and expected market return is 10%. What is the required return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 6%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 13%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 15%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>CAPM required return = 4% + 1.5(10% - 4%) = <strong>13%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Beta multiplies the market <em>risk premium</em>, not the full market return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Holding forecast cash flows constant, what happens to their present value when the required return increases?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It remains unchanged at every horizon.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It rises because the cash flows increase.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It falls because future amounts are discounted more heavily.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It becomes identical to book value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>With unchanged future cash flows, a larger discount factor reduces their present value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher required return does not itself make forecast cash flows larger.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>An analyst estimates today's intrinsic value at RM18 per share while the market price is RM15. What is the best interpretation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The share is certain to rise by RM3 tomorrow.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The estimate suggests possible undervaluation, conditional on the model and assumptions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Its realized return is guaranteed to equal the required return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The company must distribute RM18 in dividends next year.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The estimate exceeds price by RM3, suggesting a candidate for further evaluation under the chosen assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An estimated difference is not guaranteed profit.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>A stock is expected to pay a constant RM2 dividend annually forever, beginning in one year. If the required return is 10%, what is its zero-growth dividend-model value today?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM22</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM20</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Zero-growth value = $D/r=2/0.10=\\text{RM}20$, provided the first payment is one year away and the perpetuity assumptions apply.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide by the rate in decimal form.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>A company has just paid a dividend of RM2 per share. Dividends are expected to grow at 5% forever, and the required return is 10%. What is the expected dividend one year from now?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM2.10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM2.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1.90</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM2.20</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$D_1=D_0(1+g)=2(1.05)=\\text{RM}2.10$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> $D_0$ has just been paid; the next dividend is $D_1$.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Using the assumptions in Q13, what is the constant-growth dividend-model value today?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM20</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM42</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM52.50</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$P_0=D_1/(r-g)=2.10/(0.10-0.05)=\\text{RM}42$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Using $D_0$ rather than $D_1$ gives RM40.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>Which condition is required for the standard perpetual constant-growth dividend formula to produce a finite positive value under its assumptions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All dividends must be zero forever.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Dividend growth must exceed required return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Required return must equal growth.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Required return exceeds the perpetual dividend growth rate.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The perpetual dividend series converges under the standard model when $r&gt;g$, with the model's other assumptions satisfied.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The formula's algebraic appearance does not make it valid for every pair of rates.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>A stock's expected dividend next year is RM2 and the required return is 10%. What is its constant-growth value if perpetual growth is 5% instead of 4%, holding next year's dividend fixed?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It falls from RM40 to RM33.33.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It rises from approximately RM33.33 to RM40.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It remains RM20 in both cases.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It becomes infinite in both cases.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>At 4% growth: $2/(0.10-0.04)=\\text{RM}33.33$; at 5%: $2/(0.10-0.05)=\\text{RM}40$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The example holds $D_1$ fixed; if $D_0$ were fixed, $D_1$ would also change.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>For a constant-growth dividend model, the required return is 9% and assumed perpetual growth is 9%. Which response is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Divide by zero and use the result as a valid finite price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The standard formula is not valid under these inputs; reconsider the long-run assumptions or model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Apply the formula without considering assumptions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The correct stock value must be zero.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The denominator is zero and the standard perpetual formula has no finite solution under those assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A mathematical breakdown calls for revisiting the model, not assigning a convenient value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>A stock is expected to pay RM1.10 at the end of year 1 and RM1.21 at the end of year 2. It is expected to sell for RM121 at the end of year 2, immediately after the second dividend. At a 10% required return, what is its value today?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM102</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM100</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM111</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM123.31</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$P_0=1.10/1.10+(1.21+121)/1.10^2=1+101=\\text{RM}102$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Both the year-2 dividend and sale price occur in year 2; the price is explicitly ex-dividend.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>A forecast terminal stock price is estimated at the end of year 2 using a dividend model. What should be done to use it in a valuation today?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Add the terminal price directly to today's value without discounting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Discount the end-of-year-2 terminal price by two periods and add discounted dividends received before or at that date, without double counting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Discount the terminal price for one period only because it is a single amount.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Add every future dividend again even if the terminal price already represents those dividends.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A year-2 terminal value is discounted for two years. Add separately modeled dividends only when they are not already embedded in the terminal value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Terminal value is dated, and double counting overstates value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A company will pay RM1 at year 1 and RM1.20 at year 2. After year 2, dividends grow forever at 5%; required return is 10%. What is the terminal value at year 2?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM12.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM24.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM26.40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM25.20</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$D_3=1.20(1.05)=1.26$. At year 2, $P_2=D_3/(r-g)=1.26/0.05=\\text{RM}25.20$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the dividend <em>after</em> the terminal valuation date.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Using Q20, what is the stock's value today, rounded to two decimals?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM20.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM25.20</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM22.73</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM26.40</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$P_0=1/1.10+(1.20+25.20)/1.10^2\\approx0.9091+21.8182=\\text{RM}22.73$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The year-2 terminal value is not today's value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Why can a finite-horizon valuation include a future sale price even when dividends during the investor's holding period are small or zero?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The sale price is a future cash flow to the investor, but its forecast must be justified and discounted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A future share price can never be uncertain.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The sale price is necessarily equal to today's price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The sale price replaces the need to account for time and risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Expected sale proceeds are a future benefit, but the estimate needs support and must be discounted.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A terminal price is an assumption, not a guaranteed cash payment.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A company pays no dividend this year but is expected to begin dividends in five years. Which claim is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Expected later distributions can be valued, but their timing and the forecasts' uncertainty matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A dividend valuation approach must assign zero value in all circumstances.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The company has no assets because it pays no current dividend.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Growth assumptions are irrelevant when dividends begin later.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Future distributions can have positive present value even if no dividend is currently paid.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A zero current dividend does not imply that all future cash flows are zero.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>An analyst expects next year's EPS to be RM3 and selects a comparable P/E multiple of 12. What implied price is produced by this relative valuation, using consistent earnings timing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM9</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM15</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM36</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Implied price = RM3 x 12 = <strong>RM36</strong> at the date corresponding to the multiple and EPS basis.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Match the multiple to the same earnings horizon; discount a future price if comparing it with today.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Two analysts use the same expected EPS but choose different P/E multiples. Which is the most relevant explanation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Their assumptions about growth, risk or comparable companies may differ.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> P/E multiples are determined solely by the number of shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Different multiples make EPS mathematically undefined.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both implied prices are guaranteed to occur.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Multiples can differ with expected growth, risk, comparable firms and market conditions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A multiple is an assumption, not an invariant law.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>A firm has negative earnings, but positive sales. Which measure might remain usable as a relative comparison, provided comparability and margins are assessed?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A positive P/E calculated from negative EPS without adjustment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Price-to-sales</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Dividend payout ratio based on an assumed guaranteed profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Par value as a substitute for all valuation work</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Price-to-sales can be calculated when earnings are negative if sales are positive, but expected margins and comparability still matter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A calculable P/S does not establish profitability or intrinsic value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>A stock price is RM18 and cash flow per share is RM3 under a consistently defined measure. Its price-to-cash-flow ratio is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.17 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 3 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 6 times</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 15 times</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>P/CF = RM18 / RM3 = <strong>6 times</strong> under the stated cash-flow definition.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Cash-flow measures differ, so compare like with like.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A stock trades for RM12 and book value per common share is RM8. Its P/B is 1.5. What follows?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Its expected total return is exactly 1.5%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It must be overpriced by exactly 50%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It trades at 1.5 times stated book value; this alone does not establish fair value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It has no intangible assets.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>P/B = 12 / 8 = <strong>1.5 times</strong>; valuation requires context about asset quality, profitability and risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A ratio above one is not proof of overvaluation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>Which scenario illustrates <strong>sensitivity analysis</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ignore the current price because the company is profitable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Choose one forecast and treat it as certain.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Recalculate intrinsic value under reasonable alternative growth and required-return assumptions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Add two different dates' values without discounting.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Recomputing values across plausible assumptions shows how sensitive the conclusion is to inputs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Repeating one assumption does not test uncertainty.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>A model indicates RM25 intrinsic value compared with a market price of RM22, but the estimate falls below RM22 under a modest change in the growth assumption. What conclusion is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The apparent undervaluation is sensitive to assumptions; assess scenarios, risk and an appropriate margin of safety.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The RM3 gap is certain profit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The calculation eliminates forecast uncertainty.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A higher growth estimate always changes the actual dividend immediately.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A small estimated discount that disappears under a modest input change is fragile; compare scenarios and risks before acting.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Model precision is not forecast certainty.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l8p-d3\">PART B - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l8p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula and decision map</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l8_method_sketch\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Match the model to the cash-flow pattern, then check the condition that makes it valid.</figcaption></figure></div><ul>\n<li>Forecast sales = prior sales x $(1+\\text{growth})$; forecast net income = forecast sales x net margin; EPS = earnings available to common shareholders / expected common shares; dividend per share = EPS x payout.</li>\n<li>CAPM: $r=r_f+\\beta(E[R_m]-r_f)$. Finite-horizon value: $P_0=\\sum_{t=1}^{T}D_t/(1+r)^t + P_T/(1+r)^T$, with dividend and terminal-price timing consistent.</li>\n<li>Zero growth: $P_0=D/r$, first payment one period from now. Constant growth: $P_0=D_1/(r-g)$, when the model's perpetual assumptions and $r&gt;g$ hold. In a multistage model, compute terminal value at the correct date and discount it to today.</li>\n<li>Relative valuation: an appropriate multiple x consistent per-share metric gives an implied price. Match the forecast or trailing horizon and investigate comparable firms, risk and accounting differences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnose mistakes by category: forecast arithmetic; cash-flow date; wrong growth-model numerator; invalid assumptions; discount rate; multiple or units; or unsupported conclusion. For each missed question, ask which changed fact would make a different answer correct.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Original practice scenarios based on the concepts in Chapter 8, <em>Stock Valuation</em> (Pearson slides, 2014). The slides' comparison of a future forecast price with today's price must account for timing and any interim dividends to assess value at the required return. Estimates and practice targets are not guarantees or official exam requirements.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l8-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"8\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 8 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"8\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>In the constant-growth dividend valuation model $V_0 = \\dfrac{D}{k - g}$, which dividend goes in the numerator?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The dividend just paid, $D_0$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The average dividend of the past five years</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The dividend expected one year from now, $D_1$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Current earnings per share, because all earnings eventually belong to shareholders</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The formula is the present value of a stream that starts one period from now, so its first term is $D_1 = D_0(1+g)$. A is the classic trap: it values a stream one year too early and understates value. B and D are not dividends expected next year; EPS is not a dividend unless the payout is $100\\%$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>The chapter projects a company's key financial variables in three steps. Which sequence is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sales and profits, then EPS and dividends, then the stock price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Estimate beta, compute the CAPM required return, then discount next year's earnings at that rate to get the price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Compute book value per share, apply an industry P/B multiple, then compare the result with the market price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Forecast interest rates, then inflation, then GDP growth, because the economy determines every company's results</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Slide 8-6: step 1 sales and profits, step 2 EPS and dividends, step 3 the stock price (EPS times P/E). B describes setting the required return, not projecting the firm's variables. C is one relative-valuation shortcut. D lists economic inputs that feed a forecast but are not the chapter's three steps.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>The slides list several variables that influence the P/E ratio an analyst should assume. Which is <strong>not</strong> one of them?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The growth rate in earnings</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The amount of debt in the company's capital structure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Current and projected inflation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The par value of the common stock</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Slide 8-14 names earnings growth, the general state of the market, debt in the capital structure, current and projected inflation, and the level of dividends. Par value is a legal accounting figure with no link to how much investors pay per ringgit of earnings.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>An investor plans to sell a share after three years. Why can the share still be valued as the present value of <strong>all</strong> its future dividends?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because shares cannot be sold before the company is wound up, so in practice every investor collects every dividend the firm ever pays.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because the year-$3$ sale price is itself the value of the dividends after year $3$, so discounting it is discounting them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because capital gains are taxed more heavily than dividends, so the uncertain sale price is replaced by the dividends it would have produced.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because in a fair market the sale price must equal the original purchase price, so only the dividends are left to value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A terminal price summarises the value of benefits after the sale. Following the chain of buyers, every price is ultimately the present value of later dividends. That is also why adding post-sale dividends on top of the sale price double counts. A and D are false; C confuses tax with valuation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Next year's sales are forecast to rise $10\\%$, but the net profit margin is expected to fall from $8\\%$ to $6\\%$. The share count is unchanged. What happens to forecast EPS compared with last year?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It rises $10\\%$: with the share count unchanged, EPS moves one-for-one with sales whatever happens to the margin.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is unchanged, because the higher sales and the lower margin offset each other exactly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It must rise, because the margin affects operating cash flow but not reported earnings per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It falls $17.5\\%$: $1.10 \\times \\dfrac{0.06}{0.08} = 0.825$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Profit = sales x margin, so the EPS ratio is $1.10 \\times 0.06 / 0.08 = 0.825$, a $17.5\\%$ fall. Sales growth only becomes profit growth if the margin holds. A ignores the margin. B is wrong arithmetic: $10\\%$ growth does not offset a $25\\%$ cut in the margin. C misunderstands EPS, which is profit per share.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Which statement correctly distinguishes <strong>expected</strong> return from <strong>required</strong> return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Expected return is a forecast of the outcome; required return is the hurdle for the risk and the wait.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They are always equal in a competitive market, so comparing them adds nothing to a valuation exercise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Required return is the return the share actually delivered last year, used as a guide to next year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Expected return is fixed by CAPM from beta, while required return comes from the analyst's own forecasts of the dividend and the sale price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The required return is the discount rate and benchmark; the expected return comes from forecasts of $D_1$, $P_1$ and the price paid. $E(R) &gt; k$ is the same test as $V_0 &gt; P_0$. D swaps the two: CAPM gives the required return. B and C confuse the concepts with equilibrium or with realised history.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A stock has just paid $\\text{RM}1.50$. Dividends are expected to grow $6\\%$ a year forever. Its beta is $1.2$, the risk-free rate is $4\\%$ and the expected market return is $9\\%$. What is its constant-growth value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}37.50$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}18.07$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}39.75$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}15.90$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$k = 4\\% + 1.2(9\\% - 4\\%) = 10\\%$. $D_1 = 1.50(1.06) = 1.59$. $V_0 = \\dfrac{1.59}{0.10 - 0.06} = \\text{RM}39.75$. A uses $D_0$ ($1.50 / 0.04$). B uses the wrong CAPM, $4\\% + 1.2 \\times 9\\% = 14.8\\%$, giving $1.59 / 0.088$. D divides by $k$ alone, ignoring growth.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Last year's sales were $\\text{RM}250$ million. Forecast: sales growth $6\\%$, net margin $7\\%$, $5$ million shares, payout $30\\%$, year-end P/E $14$. The required return is $11\\%$. What is the share worth today, using next year's dividend and year-end price?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}51.94$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}47.80$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}53.05$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}46.79$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Sales $250(1.06) = 265$; profit $265 \\times 0.07 = 18.55$; $EPS = 18.55 / 5 = 3.71$; $D_1 = 3.71 \\times 0.30 = 1.113$; $P_1 = 3.71 \\times 14 = 51.94$. $V_0 = \\dfrac{1.113 + 51.94}{1.11} = \\text{RM}47.80$. A is the undiscounted future price; C adds the dividend but still does not discount; D discounts the price but forgets the dividend.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>$D_0 = \\text{RM}2.00$. Dividends grow $15\\%$ a year for three years, then $5\\%$ forever. The required return is $11\\%$. What is the value today?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}45.36$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}43.51$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}41.51$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}59.67$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$D_1 = 2.30$, $D_2 = 2.645$, $D_3 = 3.04175$, $D_4 = 3.04175(1.05) = 3.19384$. $P_3 = \\dfrac{3.19384}{0.11 - 0.05} = 53.23$. PV of $D_1$ to $D_3$ $= 6.443$; PV of $P_3 = 53.23 / 1.11^3 = 38.92$; total $\\text{RM}45.36$. B builds $P_3$ from $D_3$ instead of $D_4$. C discounts $P_3$ four years. D adds $P_3$ without discounting it.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Two analysts value the same stock with the constant-growth model and the same $D_1 = \\text{RM}3.00$. Analyst X uses $k = 12\\%$ and $g = 6\\%$; Analyst Y uses $k = 11\\%$ and $g = 7\\%$. What best explains the gap between their values?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Y's value is higher only because Y's required return is lower; with $D_1$ held fixed, the growth assumption has no effect.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Y must have used $D_0$ instead of $D_1$, which always produces the larger of two constant-growth values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The gap is mostly rounding: one-point changes in $k$ and $g$ move in opposite directions and largely cancel each other out.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Together the changes narrow $k - g$ from $6\\%$ to $4\\%$, lifting value from $\\text{RM}50$ to $\\text{RM}75$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$3 / 0.06 = 50$ and $3 / 0.04 = 75$. The value depends on the spread $k - g$, so two modest changes in the same direction compound. This sensitivity is exactly why the notes warn that a precise answer is not a precise forecast. A is wrong because $g$ sits in the denominator: raising it narrows the spread just as lowering $k$ does. B contradicts the stated common $D_1$. C is wrong because the two changes push the value the same way, they do not cancel.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A share trades at $\\text{RM}40$. Next year's dividend is expected to be $\\text{RM}2$ and the required return is $11\\%$. What perpetual growth rate does the price imply, and what follows if you believe growth will be only $4\\%$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $5\\%$, the dividend yield $\\dfrac{2}{40}$; at $4\\%$ growth the share therefore looks slightly underpriced compared with the market's view.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $6\\%$; at $4\\%$ growth the value is $\\dfrac{2}{0.07} = \\text{RM}28.57$, below the price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $16\\%$, the required return plus the dividend yield; the market expects explosive growth that will not last.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $11\\%$, because in a fairly priced market the implied growth rate always equals the required return.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$g = k - \\dfrac{D_1}{P_0} = 11\\% - 5\\% = 6\\%$. The price already assumes $6\\%$ growth forever; with your $4\\%$, $V_0 = 2 / (0.11 - 0.04) = \\text{RM}28.57 &lt; \\text{RM}40$. A confuses the yield with growth. C adds instead of subtracting. D would make the denominator zero.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>An analyst writes $V_0 = \\dfrac{1.00}{1.10} + \\dfrac{1.10}{1.10^2} + \\dfrac{1.155}{1.10^3} + \\dfrac{P_2}{1.10^2}$, where $P_2 = \\dfrac{D_3}{k - g}$ and $D_3 = 1.155$. What is wrong?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $P_2$ should be discounted three years, because it is built from $D_3$, which is paid at the end of year $3$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $P_2$ should be built from $D_2$, the last dividend the analyst has written out explicitly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Drop the separate $D_3$ term: $P_2$ already includes it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Nothing is wrong, but $D_4$ should also be added separately so the stable phase is complete.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$P_2$ is the year-$2$ value of $D_3, D_4, \\dots$. Adding $\\dfrac{1.155}{1.10^3} = \\text{RM}0.87$ separately overstates value by that amount. A misdates $P_2$, which is a year-$2$ value. B would value a stream that starts a year too early. D makes the double counting worse.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>With $D_1 = \\text{RM}1.25$, $k = 10\\%$ and $g = 5\\%$, a model gives $\\text{RM}25$ against a price of $\\text{RM}22$. Which evaluation is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Break-even growth is about $4.32\\%$, close to the base case, so the bargain is fragile: test scenarios and keep a margin of safety.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The break-even growth rate is $10\\%$, so the share stays undervalued under any reasonable growth assumption.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Break-even growth is $\\dfrac{1.25}{22} = 5.68\\%$; since the base case of $5\\%$ is below it, the share is already overvalued and should be sold.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Price is below value in the base case, so any positive growth rate confirms the purchase decision.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Setting $22 = 1.25 / (0.10 - g)$ gives $g = 4.32\\%$; at $g = 4.3\\%$, $V_0 = 1.25 / 0.057 = \\text{RM}21.93$, below the price. A $\\text{RM}3$ gap that disappears under a small change is fragile. C confuses the dividend yield with the break-even growth rate. B and D ignore the sensitivity.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An analyst forecasts a price of $\\text{RM}60$ in three years, with no dividends. The share costs $\\text{RM}45$ and the required return is $12\\%$. The analyst says: \"$45 &lt; 60$, so it is undervalued.\" Evaluate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Agree: the investor gains $\\text{RM}15$, a $33\\%$ return, which is comfortably more than the $12\\%$ hurdle.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Agree: discounted at $12\\%$ the future price is still $\\dfrac{60}{1.12} = \\text{RM}53.57$, well above $\\text{RM}45$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It cannot be judged until beta has been estimated and CAPM has been used to recompute the required return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Disagree: $\\dfrac{60}{1.12^3} = \\text{RM}42.71 &lt; 45$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The $\\text{RM}60$ arrives in three years, so it must be discounted three years. A compares a three-year total return with an annual hurdle. B discounts only one year. C: the required return is already given, so no beta is needed.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Delta's multiples give implied prices of $\\text{RM}30$ (P/E), $\\text{RM}32$ (P/CF), $\\text{RM}24$ (P/S) and $\\text{RM}25$ (P/B); the price is $\\text{RM}28$. Delta's peers have <strong>less debt and faster growth</strong> than Delta. Which evaluation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Averaging the four estimates gives $\\text{RM}27.75$, so Delta is overvalued by exactly $\\text{RM}0.25$ and the multiples settle the question.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The P/E and P/CF estimates probably overstate Delta's value, so $\\text{RM}28$ proves nothing yet: adjust or cross-check.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> P/CF is the most reliable multiple because cash flow cannot be manipulated, so Delta is undervalued by $\\text{RM}4$ and should be bought.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only P/B matters because book value is audited, so Delta is overvalued by $\\text{RM}3$ whatever the other multiples say.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Multiples carry the peers' growth and risk. With more debt and slower growth, Delta deserves lower multiples, which weakens the higher estimates. A treats an average of non-comparable estimates as precise. C and D pick one ratio by decree; no multiple is universally best.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A firm pays no dividend now. It is expected to pay $\\text{RM}1.00$ at the end of year $4$, growing $5\\%$ a year forever after that. The required return is $12\\%$. Which plan and value are right?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Its value is zero, because a dividend model can only value dividends paid within the next three years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Its value is $\\dfrac{1.00}{0.12 - 0.05} = \\text{RM}14.29$ today, because the growing-perpetuity formula already accounts for timing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Value the stream at year $3$: $P_3 = \\dfrac{1.00}{0.07} = 14.29$; then $V_0 = \\dfrac{14.29}{1.12^3} \\approx \\text{RM}10.17$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Value the stream at year $3$ but discount it four years, because the first dividend is not paid until year $4$: about $\\text{RM}9.08$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The growing-perpetuity formula gives a value one period before the first payment. With $D_4$ first, that is year $3$. Then discount $14.2857 / 1.404928 = 10.17$. B treats a year-$3$ value as today's. D discounts one year too many. A ignores later distributions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>In your two-stage valuation, about $85\\%$ of the value comes from the terminal value. Which monitoring plan fits best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Track long-run growth and $k$; revalue when either moves past a preset trigger.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Track next quarter's dividend closely, because it is the earliest and most certain cash flow and so carries the largest weight in present value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Watch only the share price and sell after any $10\\%$ fall, since the price already reflects every change in the assumptions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No monitoring is needed: once the formula has produced a value, the value is exact until the next annual report.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>When most of the value sits in the terminal value, the long-run growth rate and $k$ drive the answer, so those assumptions deserve explicit review triggers. B monitors the input that matters least. C reacts to price without asking whether the thesis changed. D confuses arithmetic precision with forecast certainty.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You build three scenarios for a stock with $D_1 = \\text{RM}2$ and $k = 10\\%$: growth $3\\%$ (probability $25\\%$), $4\\%$ ($50\\%$) and $5\\%$ ($25\\%$). What should you report?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}33.33$ only: the average growth rate is $4\\%$, so the scenario values average back to the base case and add nothing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}40$, because investors are most interested in how much they could make in the bull case.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}28.57$, because a prudent valuation should always report the worst case and nothing else.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About $\\text{RM}33.81$ probability-weighted, with a range of $\\text{RM}28.57$ to $\\text{RM}40$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Scenario values are $2 / 0.07 = 28.57$, $2 / 0.06 = 33.33$ and $2 / 0.05 = 40$. Weighted: $0.25(28.57) + 0.5(33.33) + 0.25(40) = 33.81$. Because $V$ is convex in $g$, the average of the values exceeds the value at the average growth rate, so A understates. Reporting the range shows how fragile the estimate is. B and C each report one tail only.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>An analyst sets a target for a loss-making software firm using a price-to-sales multiple from peers. Why, and what is the main limitation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> P/S is always more accurate than P/E, because sales cannot be managed by accounting choices the way earnings can.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> P/S measures profitability directly, so it shows how far the firm is from breaking even and when it will get there.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> P/E is meaningless with negative EPS; sales still give scale, but they do not show whether margins will ever turn positive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> P/S automatically adjusts for the firm's debt and capital structure, so no further comparability checks are needed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The notes recommend P/S when earnings are weak or negative, with the warning that sales do not reveal sustainable margins or cash flow. A overstates the case (revenue recognition can be managed too). B is false: P/S says nothing about profit. D confuses price-to-sales with enterprise-value-to-sales: neither removes the need to check capital structure.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A sell-side note forecasts next year's EPS at $\\text{RM}2.40$, a target P/E of $16$ and a dividend of $\\text{RM}0.80$. The share costs $\\text{RM}34$ and your required return is $12\\%$. Which summary is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The target is $\\text{RM}38.40$, an upside of $12.9\\%$ on today's price, which beats $12\\%$, so the dividend can be ignored in the decision.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $V_0 = \\dfrac{0.80 + 38.40}{1.12} = \\text{RM}35.00 &gt; 34$, and $E(R) = 15.29\\% &gt; 12\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $V_0 = \\dfrac{38.40}{1.12} = \\text{RM}34.29$, so the share is fairly priced at $\\text{RM}34$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The value today is $\\text{RM}39.20$, the target price plus next year's dividend.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$2.40 \\times 16 = 38.40$ is a year-end price. Adding the dividend and discounting gives $39.20 / 1.12 = 35.00$, and both tests agree. A happens to reach a similar verdict but ignores the dividend and the same-date logic; with a $13\\%$ hurdle its reasoning would mislead. C omits the dividend. D does not discount.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A firm's next-year EPS is $\\text{RM}4.00$ and its payout ratio is $40\\%$. Its return on equity is $15\\%$, so sustainable growth is $g = ROE \\times (1 - \\text{payout})$. The risk-free rate is $3\\%$, beta $0.9$ and the market risk premium $6\\%$. What does the constant-growth model give?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> No meaningful value: $k = 8.4\\% &lt; g = 9\\%$, so this growth cannot last forever.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $V_0 = \\dfrac{1.60}{0.084 - 0.09} = -\\text{RM}266.67$: the model says the share is worth less than nothing, so it should be sold short.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $V_0 = \\dfrac{1.60}{0.084} = \\text{RM}19.05$, treating the dividend as level because growth cannot be estimated reliably.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $V_0 = \\dfrac{4.00}{0.09 - 0.084} = \\text{RM}666.67$, because all earnings ultimately belong to shareholders.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$D_1 = 4.00 \\times 0.40 = 1.60$, $k = 8.4\\%$, $g = 9\\%$. With $k &lt; g$ the perpetual series does not converge; a negative denominator does not mean a negative value (B). C drops growth; D uses EPS and reverses the denominator. The right response is to recognise that $9\\%$ growth must fade.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>From the constant-growth model, the justified forward P/E is $\\dfrac{P_0}{E_1} = \\dfrac{\\text{payout}}{k - g}$. With payout $50\\%$, $k = 10\\%$ and $g = 5\\%$, what is the justified P/E, and what does a market P/E of $14$ imply?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $14\\times$ is justified, because a P/E is set by the market and does not depend on the firm's growth or risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The justified P/E is $\\dfrac{1}{0.05} = 20\\times$, so a market multiple of $14\\times$ means the share is cheap.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The justified P/E is $\\dfrac{0.5}{0.10} = 5\\times$, so the market is paying almost three times too much for the earnings.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $10\\times$; a $14\\times$ market multiple implies $g \\approx 6.43\\%$ or a lower $k$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$0.5 / (0.10 - 0.05) = 10$. Solving $0.5 / (0.10 - g) = 14$ gives $g \\approx 6.43\\%$. So the market multiple embeds faster growth or lower risk than the analyst's inputs. B forgets the payout ($1 / 0.05$). C divides payout by $k$ alone. A ignores the link between multiples, growth and risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A team's two-stage model has a present value of explicit dividends of $\\text{RM}6.20$ and a present value of the year-$5$ terminal value of $\\text{RM}31.80$, using $k = 9\\%$ and $g = 3\\%$. The price is $\\text{RM}33$. A judge asks what happens if $k$ is $10\\%$. Holding the year-$6$ dividend fixed, what happens to the terminal value's present value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It falls by one ninth, to $\\text{RM}28.27$, because a rise from $9\\%$ to $10\\%$ is a one-ninth rise in the discount rate and value is proportional to it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It falls to about $\\text{RM}26.04$, so total value (about $\\text{RM}32.24$) drops below the $\\text{RM}33$ price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It does not change, because the year-$6$ dividend is unchanged and only the explicit dividends are discounted at the new rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It rises, because a higher required return means investors expect a higher return from holding the share.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Terminal value at year $5$: $31.80 \\times 1.09^5 = 48.93$, so $D_6 = 48.93 \\times 0.06 = 2.936$. At $10\\%$: $P_5 = 2.936 / 0.07 = 41.94$, worth $41.94 / 1.10^5 = 26.04$ today. Both the narrower spread and heavier discounting hit it. A treats the effect as proportional; C ignores discounting; D confuses required with expected return.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Under constant growth with $D_1 = \\text{RM}2.08$, $k = 10\\%$ and $g = 4\\%$, $P_0 = \\text{RM}34.67$. If the model holds, what is the expected price in one year and the expected one-year return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $P_1 = \\text{RM}36.05$; return $= 6\\% + 4\\% = 10\\%$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $P_1 = \\text{RM}34.67$, because a constant-growth share has a constant price; the expected return is the $6\\%$ dividend yield alone.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $P_1 = \\text{RM}36.05$, and the expected return is $4\\%$, because only the change in price counts as return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $P_1 = \\text{RM}37.49$, and the expected return is $14\\%$, because growth compounds on both the dividend and the price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>In the model the price grows at $g$, so $E(R) = \\dfrac{D_1}{P_0} + g = 6\\% + 4\\% = 10\\%$: a correctly priced share is expected to earn exactly $k$. B forgets that dividends grow. C ignores the dividend. D compounds the growth twice and double counts.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Consider a dividend stream with $D_1 = \\text{RM}2.08$ growing at $g = 10\\%$, discounted at $k = 10\\%$. What is the present value of the first $50$ dividends, and what does it show?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It converges to $\\dfrac{D_1}{k} = \\text{RM}20.80$, because once growth equals the discount rate the two simply cancel out of the formula.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $2.08 \\times 50 = \\text{RM}104$, because when $g = k$ growth and discounting both disappear and each dividend counts at face value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is negative, because the denominator $k - g$ is zero and the formula breaks down.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About $\\text{RM}94.55$, and it grows without limit as the horizon lengthens.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Term $t$ is $\\dfrac{D_1(1+g)^{t-1}}{(1+k)^t} = \\dfrac{D_1}{1+k}$ when $g = k$. Each extra year adds $\\text{RM}1.89$, without end. This is the mechanism behind the $k &gt; g$ condition, and why a firm cannot grow dividends as fast as the discount rate forever. A and B mis-sum the series; C confuses a formula breakdown with a negative value.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A team pitches \"buy\" because the target trades at $8\\times$ earnings while peers trade at $12\\times$. Which judge's challenge is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> P/E below $10$ signals undervaluation in almost every market, so the pitch is sound and the judges should accept it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Peer multiples are irrelevant once the target's own P/E has been calculated from its audited earnings.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A low P/E may reflect higher risk, weaker growth or one-off earnings: normalise EPS and test the peers first.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Multiply the peers' $12\\times$ by the target's EPS to find the price the target is guaranteed to reach within a year.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The notes stress that a low multiple alone does not prove undervaluation; the market may be pricing risk or growth correctly, or EPS may be unrepresentative. A and D treat a multiple as a law; B discards the comparison that gives the multiple meaning.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A firm pays only $10\\%$ of earnings as dividends but returns a lot of cash through regular share buybacks. An analyst values it with a constant-growth DVM using today's dividend. Evaluate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Appropriate: buybacks never change what a remaining shareholder receives, so the dividend is the full picture of cash returned.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risky: the dividend understates cash returned; model the buybacks or total payout per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Appropriate, because buybacks always destroy value, so leaving them out of the model is simply conservative.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use P/B instead, because book value automatically captures every buyback and is therefore unaffected by payout policy.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The notes warn that a low current dividend is not a complete description of future shareholder benefits, and that share-count changes affect per-share figures. A and C are false generalisations. D swaps one incomplete measure for another; buybacks reduce book equity, which can distort P/B.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>A share trades at $\\text{RM}100$ with $D_1 = \\text{RM}2$ and $k = 9\\%$, so the implied perpetual growth is $g = 9\\% - 2\\% = 7\\%$. Long-run nominal economic growth is believed to be around $4\\%$ to $5\\%$. Evaluate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The market price is always right, so $7\\%$ growth forever is the best available forecast and should be used in the analyst's own model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The implied growth rate is irrelevant: what matters is only whether the dividend yield is above the risk-free rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Growth of $7\\%$ forever is implausible, so the price implies fast growth that fades, or a lower $k$: scrutinise it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The price must fall soon to the value at $4\\%$ growth, $\\dfrac{2}{0.05} = \\text{RM}40$, as the market corrects its error.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Reverse-engineering the price shows what it assumes. A firm growing faster than the economy forever would eventually outgrow it, so a two-stage reading or a different $k$ is more credible. A is uncritical; B discards the most useful output of the exercise; D treats a model value as a forecast of the timing and size of a price move.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>A working paper claims: \"Any dividend or cash-flow model in which more than $80\\%$ of value comes from the terminal value is uninformative and should be discarded.\" Evaluate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct: such models should be replaced by a P/E multiple, which values the firm without any terminal assumption at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Partly: report sensitivities and cross-checks, but a high terminal share is normal for growing firms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Wrong: the terminal value comes from a formula, so it is the most reliable part of any multi-stage model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Wrong: if the required return comes from CAPM, the share of value in the terminal value does not matter.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>In the notes' two-stage example the terminal value is about $87\\%$ of value, simply because most cash arrives later. The right response is transparency and stress testing, not rejection. A forgets that a multiple embeds the same long-run assumptions implicitly. C and D confuse a formula with certainty.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>You must value a newly listed firm with negative earnings and no dividend. Which protocol is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scenario forecasts of sales, margins and dilution, a terminal value with $g &lt; k$, a value range, and a P/S cross-check.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Apply the peer group's average P/E to the firm's negative EPS, since a market multiple already captures growth, risk and dilution.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Set the value to zero until the first dividend is paid, because a dividend model cannot value a firm that pays nothing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Take the IPO offer price as intrinsic value, since the underwriters have already done the analysis on the road show.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>It follows the chapter's chain (forecast the business → per-share benefits → timing and risk → value → compare) and adds cross-checks and sensitivity. B yields a meaningless negative price. C ignores later distributions. D confuses an offer price, set partly by demand on the road show, with an independent estimate of value.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
